+# Answer your agents from your phone
+
+A Projector iPhone app shows the console's sessions, pull requests, and
+inbox items, and lets you answer and discuss them. It needs only your
+GitHub credentials and your relay key, and it reaches your machines through
+the relay's hidden refs. A small push service, which never sees what an
+item says, wakes the phone when an agent needs you.
+
+## 1. Problem
+
+`project console` (the parent project, `console`) lets you answer and
+discuss every live session's items, but only in a terminal on the machine
+those sessions run on. When you step away, your agents wait. A question
+posted while you are out of reach stalls its session until you come back to
+a terminal.
+
+The pieces for a remote view already exist:
+
+- **The relay** carries agents' presence and messages between your
+  machines through hidden refs in a GitHub repository they share. Every
+  record is sealed with your relay key, so the repository holds only
+  ciphertext.
+- **GitHub's API** can read and write those refs, so a phone with a GitHub
+  token can reach them without Git.
+- **`project agent key join` and `key grant`** already copy the relay key
+  to a new machine without showing it.
+
+The relay is built for agents, though, not for you. It carries no inbox
+items, and by design a relay message carries none of your authority: every
+machine holds the relay key, and an agent running as you can read it.
+
+## 2. Solution
+
+The watch daemon publishes each live session's state to the relay, and the
+app reads it, draws the console's home list and panels, and writes your
+answers back as signed records. Three changes make that safe:
+
+- **The phone signs as you.** The phone holds a P-256 signing key in its
+  Secure Enclave, the chip that makes and uses the key and never lets it
+  out, not even into a backup. You register its public key on each machine
+  in a terminal. The daemon records a phone record as yours only when that key
+  signed it, so no agent can forge an answer, even one that reads the relay
+  key.
+- **The daemon publishes sessions and items.** For each live session it
+  already relays, the daemon publishes the session's open inbox items and
+  its pull request subscriptions, sealed like every relay record.
+- **The daemon imports your answers.** It fetches the phone's records,
+  checks each signature, and applies it with the same inbox functions the
+  console calls. The session's own deliverer then brings it in: the panel
+  for Claude Code, and the Codex adapter for Codex.
+- **A push service wakes the phone.** When a session posts an item, the
+  daemon seals a notification to the phone's key and asks the push service
+  to forward it. The service holds Apple's push credentials and an opaque
+  push ID for the phone, and nothing else. The phone's notification service
+  extension opens the sealed text on the device, so the service never sees
+  what the item says.
+
+The app, in `ios/`, and the push service, in `services/push/`, live in this
+repository beside the protocol they share, so one pull request changes the
+daemon, the app, and their test vectors together. Being open source, they
+let anyone read the code that holds their GitHub token and their keys, and
+run the push service themselves.
+
+### 2.1 Flow
+
+1. **Set up the phone once.**
+   1. Sign in to GitHub in the app, with GitHub's device flow. The token
+      needs read and write access to the repositories your sessions work
+      in.
+   2. Join the relay key. The app makes a temporary key pair and shows a
+      `project agent key grant …` command and a fingerprint, exactly as
+      `key join` does. You run the command on a machine that has the key,
+      check the fingerprint, and paste its one-line answer into the app.
+   3. Register with the push service. The app sends Apple's device token
+      to the service, which answers with a random push ID.
+   4. Make the phone's keys, and register them. The Secure Enclave makes
+      the P-256 signing key, which signs your answers. Using it needs Face
+      ID or the passcode, which the app asks for at most once every 5
+      minutes. The app makes an X25519 push key in the Keychain, which the
+      notification service extension can read while the phone is locked,
+      after its first unlock. The app shows both public keys and the push ID as one
+      `project agent phone add …` command with a fingerprint. You run it in
+      a terminal on each machine whose agents the phone may answer. The
+      command refuses without a terminal, as `key grant` does.
+2. **The daemon publishes.** On each relay pass, for each live session with
+   a relay presence, the daemon writes one sealed record per open inbox
+   item, and one sealed record of the session's subscriptions with their
+   roles. It deletes a record once its item resolves or its session lapses.
+3. **The daemon notifies.** For each item it publishes for the first time,
+   and each agent reply to a discussion, the daemon posts one notification
+   to the push service for each registered phone. The notification's text,
+   the session's label and the item's subject, is sealed to that phone's
+   push key. The service forwards it to Apple's push service with the
+   `mutable-content` flag, and the phone's notification service extension
+   opens it, so the lock screen shows the subject and the service never
+   sees it.
+4. **The app reads.** When you open it, and on background refresh, the app
+   lists the key's refs in each repository it knows, opens the records, and
+   asks GitHub directly for each subscribed pull request's state, checks,
+   and unresolved threads. It turns those into rows with the same rules as
+   the console, and draws the home list and each session's panel. Opening
+   a notification opens that item's drawer.
+5. **You answer.** The app writes your choice as an answer record, signed
+   with the phone's key and then sealed. It holds one action: an answer,
+   `done`, `decline`, `dismiss`, `snooze`, `restore`, `start`, `unclear`,
+   `read`, or a `discuss` message, with the item's reference and the
+   item's content digest, so an answer to an item that has since changed
+   is refused.
+6. **The daemon imports.** On its next pass, the daemon on the session's
+   machine fetches the record, checks the seal, then the signature against
+   the registered phone keys, and then that the record is fresh and was not
+   seen before. It applies the action with the inbox function the console
+   calls, deletes the ref, and republishes the item. A record that fails a
+   check is rejected and logged, as the relay logs rejections today.
+7. **The session hears it.** The session's deliverer claims the new line
+   and brings it in as a new turn, as it does for an answer from the
+   console.
+
+### 2.2 New relay records
+
+| Ref | Written by | Holds |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `refs/projector/items/<key-id>/<relay-id>/<ref>` | The daemon | One open inbox item, its digest, and its thread |
+| `refs/projector/subscriptions/<key-id>/<relay-id>` | The daemon | The session's pull requests and its role on each |
+| `refs/projector/answers/<key-id>/<relay-id>/<id>` | The phone | One signed action on one item, or a `discuss` message that starts a discussion |
+
+Each record keeps the relay's limits: a parentless commit whose tree holds
+one `sealed.json` of at most 32 KiB, sealed to the key's own public key.
+An answer record also carries, inside the seal:
+
+| Field | Value |
+| --- | --- |
+| `phone` | The ID of the phone key that signed it |
+| `item` | The item's reference and its digest when the phone read it |
+| `action` | The action and its argument, such as the letter or the message |
+| `at` | When the phone wrote it. The daemon rejects a record older than 7 days, as the relay does for messages |
+| `nonce` | A random ID. The daemon remembers the IDs it applied for 7 days and rejects a repeat |
+| `signature` | The phone's ECDSA P-256 signature, over SHA-256, of every field above in canonical JSON: keys sorted, no spaces, UTF-8 |
+
+### 2.3 The push service
+
+The push service is a small HTTPS service in `services/push/`, with two
+calls and no storage. It is TypeScript on Node, whose standard library has
+the HTTP/2 client that Apple's push service requires and the ES256 signing
+its tokens use, so it has no runtime dependencies at all. It deploys on its
+own, apart from the `projector.bot` site in `web/`, as a Vercel project
+whose root directory is `services/push/`. Its two calls are Vercel
+Functions on the Node runtime. The app uses this hosted instance by
+default.
+
+| Call | Sent by | Body | What the service does |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| `POST /v1/register` | The app | Apple's device token | Encrypts the token with the service's own secret key and returns the result as the push ID |
+| `POST /v1/push` | The daemon | The push ID and the sealed notification, at most 2 KiB | Decrypts the push ID to the device token and forwards the sealed text to it through Apple's push service, and answers `202`. It answers `404` for a push ID it cannot decrypt or that Apple reports as unregistered, and `429` past 60 notifications an hour for one push ID |
+
+A push ID is the device token itself, encrypted and authenticated so that
+only the service can read it and no one can forge one. So the service keeps
+no table and needs no database, and losing its host loses nothing but its
+secret key and Apple's push key, both from its environment. It stores no
+notification and logs no body. It counts each push ID's notifications in
+memory for the rate limit. Vercel runs and retires function instances as
+traffic comes and goes, so that count is a best-effort cap for each
+instance, not a shared one. Vercel's Runtime Cache can make it shared
+later, still without a database. A push ID reaches the
+daemon only inside `project agent phone add`, so knowing one is the only
+credential `POST /v1/push` asks for, and the rate limit caps what a leaked
+ID can do. `phone.push_url` in `~/.projector.toml` names the service, so
+anyone can run their own.
+
