+
+## 3. Why this design
+
+- **The phone's own key carries your authority.** The relay key is on
+  every machine, and an agent can read it there, so a record sealed with it
+  says nothing about who wrote it. A key that exists only on the phone, and
+  that you register in a terminal, keeps the rule that agents cannot answer
+  for you.
+- **The signing key lives in the Secure Enclave.** A key there can never be
+  copied off the phone, not by malware on the phone and not into a backup,
+  so a signed answer means this phone, unlocked, with your face or
+  passcode. The Secure Enclave supports only P-256, and Python's standard
+  library has no elliptic-curve signatures, so `crypto.py` gains a
+  pure-Python ECDSA P-256 verifier, as it already implements X25519 and
+  ChaCha20 itself. Verifying uses only public values, so the timing leaks
+  that make hand-written signing code dangerous do not apply, and
+  Wycheproof's test vectors pin its edge cases. Ed25519 in the Keychain
+  would be simpler to verify, but a Keychain key can be backed up and
+  restored onto another device.
+- **The push key lives in the Keychain.** The Secure Enclave cannot hold an
+  X25519 key, and the notification service extension must open a
+  notification while the phone is locked. The push key only reveals
+  notification text, never your authority, so a Keychain key readable after
+  the first unlock is enough.
+- **A lost phone is removed, not recovered.** The signing key cannot leave
+  the phone, so a new phone gets new keys. You run
+  `project agent phone remove` for the old phone and `project agent phone
+  add` for the new one on each machine.
+- **GitHub carries the records, and the push service only wakes the
+  phone.** The relay already moves records through GitHub, so a server that
+  stored items or answers would add hosting, accounts, and a second place
+  your data lives. Instant notifications need Apple's push service, which
+  only a server holding Apple's credentials can call. The push service is
+  the smallest server that can: it learns that some phone got a
+  notification, when, and from which address, but never what it says,
+  because the daemon seals the text to the phone. Without it, the app could
+  only refresh when iOS grants background time, which can be many minutes
+  apart, and a waiting agent stalls that long.
+- **The daemon imports answers, not the deliverers.** Applying an action
+  with the console's inbox functions means the phone, the console, and the
+  terminal all record the same thing, and the panel and the Codex adapter
+  deliver it unchanged.
+- **One record per item.** A session's items together can pass the relay's
+  32 KiB limit, and one record per item lets the daemon delete a resolved
+  item without rewriting the rest.
+- **An answer names the item's digest.** An agent can revise an item while
+  your answer is on its way. The digest makes the daemon refuse an answer
+  to text you never saw, as `unclear` already checks the text you read.
+- **The app and the push service are open source.** The app holds your
+  GitHub token, your relay key, and the key that answers for you, and the
+  service sees when you are notified. Users can trust them only if they can
+  read them, build the app, and run the service themselves.
+- **One repository.** The daemon, the app, and the service share one
+  protocol and one set of test vectors. Separate repositories would make
+  each protocol change a pair of pull requests that have to land in step,
+  and would copy the fixtures between them. In one repository, a change and
+  every side of it get one review, and the app's tests read the fixtures in
+  place. Swift needs macOS runners, so the app and the service get their own
+  workflows, each run only when its directory or the fixtures change, and
+  the packaging tests keep both out of everything Projector ships.
+- **The push service stores nothing.** A push ID that is the encrypted
+  device token lets the service run with no database: it can be redeployed,
+  moved, or lost without losing anyone's registration, and it holds nothing
+  worth stealing beyond its two keys.
+- **The push service deploys apart from the site.** Serving it as routes
+  of `projector.bot` would save one deploy, but the code holding Apple's
+  push key and the service's secret would then run inside the Next.js
+  process, beside every package the site depends on. No one could audit
+  that, and a compromised dependency anywhere in the site could read both
+  keys. On its own, the service is a few hundred lines with no
+  dependencies, which anyone can read whole, and it is redeployed only when
+  it changes, not with each change to the site's pages or dependencies.
+  Because it stores nothing, the separate deploy costs one small host or
+  function and two environment variables.
+- **The row model is shared again.** The app computes rows from GitHub's
+  answers, as the console computes them from the daemon's cache. The
+  fixtures in `tests/fixtures/rows/` run through the app's model too, so a
+  third copy cannot drift.
+
+## 4. Acceptance criteria
+
+| Do | Expect | Catches |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Post a question in a relayed session | Within one relay pass, an item record appears under the session's relay ID, sealed, and it opens to the item | Items that never reach the phone |
+| Resolve the item from the console | The item record is deleted on the next pass | Answered items that stay on the phone |
+| Write a signed answer record for item 3 | Item 3 is answered as the record says, the ref is deleted, and the session's deliverer sends `ANSWER 3.a …` | An answer that never reaches the inbox |
+| Write the same record a second time | The daemon rejects it as a repeat, and item 3 is unchanged | A replayed answer |
+| Write an answer record sealed correctly but signed with an unregistered key | Rejected and logged, and item 3 stays open | An agent that answers for you with the relay key |
+| Revise item 3, then apply an answer that names its old digest | Rejected as stale, and item 3 stays open | An answer to text you never saw |
+| Run `project agent phone add` without a terminal | Exit 69, nothing registered | An agent that registers its own key |
+| Seal and sign each fixture in `tests/fixtures/relay/` in Python and in the app | Both produce records the other opens and verifies | A seal or signature that only one side can read |
+| Run Wycheproof's ECDSA P-256 SHA-256 vectors through `crypto.py`'s verifier | Every vector marked valid verifies, and every one marked invalid is rejected | A verifier that accepts a malformed or forged signature |
+| Verify a signature the Secure Enclave made on a test device, saved in `tests/fixtures/relay/` | It verifies, and flipping any byte of the record or the signature makes it fail | A signature encoding, such as DER against raw, that Python and the phone disagree on |
+| Run each fixture in `tests/fixtures/rows/` through the app's row model | The fixture's expected rows | A third row model that drifts |
+| Post a question with a phone registered, against a stand-in push service that records every request | One `POST /v1/push` with the phone's push ID, whose body holds neither the subject nor the session's label in plain text, and the phone's push key opens it to both | A notification that leaks the item to the service, or one the phone cannot open |
+| Make the push service answer `500`, or not answer for 30 seconds | The relay pass and every session's delivery go on, and the daemon logs the failure once | A push failure that stalls the daemon |
+| Send 61 notifications to one push ID in an hour | The service answers `429` to the 61st | A leaked push ID that can flood the phone |
+
+## 5. Cost
+
+- **GitHub quota on the phone.** A refresh lists the key's refs in each
+  repository, reads each new record, and asks GraphQL for each subscribed
+  pull request. With ten sessions and thirty pull requests, measure one
+  refresh at under 50 REST calls and 5 GraphQL points, well inside the
+  5,000 requests an hour a token gets.
+- **The daemon's pushes.** Items change rarely, so publishing adds a few
+  refs to the one push each relay pass already makes.
+- **The push service.** It handles one request per new item or reply, a
+  few hundred a day for a busy user, and stores nothing. Each call is one
+  short function invocation on Vercel, so many users fit in a small plan.
+  Apple charges nothing for push, but sending it needs an Apple developer
+  account's push key.
+- **What a leaked relay key exposes.** A deleted ref's commit stays
+  readable by its SHA on GitHub. Inbox items hold more detail than presence
+  records, so a leaked key exposes every item the relay published. The
+  documentation says so, as it does for messages today.
+
+## 6. Open questions
+
+- **How the phone finds repositories.** The relay works per repository,
+  and the phone cannot list every repository that holds your refs without
+  probing each one. Options: the grant line carries the repositories the
+  granting machine relays, or the daemon publishes a roster to a home
+  repository you name. Owner: whoever takes this project.
+- **The push service's domain.** The hosted instance runs on Vercel. It
+  can use the project's default domain or a subdomain of `projector.bot`.
+  Owner: the user, when the service is first deployed.
+- **App Store distribution.** Publishing needs an Apple developer account
+  and App Review. TestFlight can come first. Owner: the user.
+- **Messages to agents.** The phone could also send relay messages to an
+  agent, as `project agent send` does. They would carry none of your
+  authority, so the first version sends only signed answers and
+  discussion. Deferred.
+
+## 7. Background
+
+The relay's design is in `src/projector/relay.py`'s module docstring and in
+`docs/cli.md` under "Message agents on other machines". Records are sealed
+with ChaCha20 and HMAC-SHA256 under a key agreed with X25519, in
+`src/projector/crypto.py`, which implements them in pure Python because the
+CLI has no runtime dependencies. Apple's CryptoKit provides X25519 and
+ChaCha20-Poly1305, but not the relay's ChaCha20-and-HMAC construction, so
+the app implements that construction itself and checks it against the
+shared fixtures.
